<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goujian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 465 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as King Goujian of Yue</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Goujian was king of the State of Yue from 496 to 465 BCE. Defeated by King Fuchai of Wu in 494 BCE, he served him as a near-hostage, then rebuilt Yue with his ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wen-zhong">Wen Zhong</a>, by tradition sleeping on brushwood and tasting gall to keep his humiliation in mind. He destroyed Wu in 473 BCE. Later rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, including the kings of Minyue and Dong'ou, claimed descent from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goujian.jpg" alt="Goujian" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gisling · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%B6%8A%E7%8E%8B%E5%8F%B0.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
